Fix xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland leaking one EIS socket, and one dead session
entry, for every input-capture client that goes away.

Root cause, two halves in src/portals/InputCapture.cpp:

  1. SSession stored the EIS fd handed over by hyprland-input-capture-v1 as a
     raw `int32_t eisFD` and never closed it. There was no close() anywhere in
     the file and SSession had no destructor. A wayland fd event transfers
     ownership to the receiver, so that fd is the portal's to close.
  2. removeSession() had no callers at all. session->onDestroy only set
     `dead = true`, leaving the entry in m_sessions, so the map grew without
     bound for the lifetime of the portal.

Why it is worse than an ordinary fd leak: libei's EIS server writes its device
advertisement (roughly one keymap fd per input device) into the socket as soon
as the client connects. A client that dies before reading leaves that whole
batch queued, and queued SCM_RIGHTS fds count as IN FLIGHT against the per-UID
AF_UNIX budget. net/unix/scm.c too_many_unix_fds() then fails any further
SCM_RIGHTS sendmsg() with ETOOMANYREFS for every process whose soft
RLIMIT_NOFILE is below that count -- which is systemd's default 1024, i.e.
almost everything. Worse, the queues hold fds referring to other unix sockets,
so they are reference cycles that only unix_gc() can collect, and the kernel
only runs that sweep above UNIX_INFLIGHT_TRIGGER_GC (16000). A few thousand
stranded fds therefore sit below the collection threshold permanently: pinned
until reboot.

Measured on thanatos 2026-08-07 with a crash-looping input-capture client
(kdeconnect_shareinputdevices, 88 core dumps in ~90s): 93 leaked EIS sockets,
88 of them holding exactly 7160 unread bytes, pinning ~2470 fds. Since Hyprland
hands its children the original soft limit (1024) while running at 524288
itself, every GUI app launched from a keybind then died at Wayland connect with
"[glfw error 65544]: Wayland: fatal display error: Too many references: cannot
splice" -- presenting as "keybinds stopped working", not as an fd problem.

Fix: close eisFD in onDestroy (and in a new ~SSession as a backstop for
teardown paths where onDestroy never fires), prune dead sessions from
m_sessions, and defensively close a previous fd if the compositor ever delivers
a second one for the same session.

Applies to b653ab53 (the rev pinned in helpers/overlays.nix) and to master
688feb3 -- InputCapture.{cpp,hpp} are identical between them.

Not upstreamed yet.

